text Errors involving known interoperability problems in legacy user agents @17,33889,694,20
text Certain syntax constructs are known to cause especially subtle or serious problems in legacy user agents, and are therefore marked as non-conforming to help authors avoid them. @17,33921,694,40
text For example, this is why the U+0060 GRAVE ACCENT character (`) is not allowed in unquoted attributes. In certain legacy user agents, it is sometimes treated as a quote character. @17,33973,694,41
text Another example of this is the DOCTYPE, which is required to trigger , because the behavior of legacy user agents in is often largely undocumented. @17,34026,694,41
link "no-quirks mode" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-no-quirks @17,34079,694,20
link "quirks mode" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-quirks @17,34111,694,20
text Errors that risk exposing authors to security attacks @17,34143,694,21
text Certain restrictions exist purely to avoid known security problems. @17,34176,694,20
text For example, the restriction on using UTF-7 exists purely to avoid authors falling prey to a known cross-site-scripting attack using UTF-7. @17,34208,694,40
link [UTF7] references.html#refsUTF7 @17,34260,694,21
text Cases where the author's intent is unclear @17,34293,694,20
text Markup where the author's intent is very unclear is often made non-conforming. Correcting these errors early makes later maintenance easier. @17,34325,694,41
text For example, it is unclear whether the author intended the following to be an heading or an heading: @17,34378,694,20
row @17,34410,694,20
