text Errors that would likely result in scripts failing in hard-to-debug ways @17,39120,694,20
text Some errors are intended to help prevent script problems that would be hard to debug. @17,39152,694,21
text This is why, for instance, it is non-conforming to have two attributes with the same value. Duplicate IDs lead to the wrong element being selected, with sometimes disastrous effects whose cause is hard to determine. @17,39185,694,61
link id dom.html#the-id-attribute @17,39258,694,20
text Errors that waste authoring time @17,39290,694,20
text Some constructs are disallowed because historically they have been the cause of a lot of wasted authoring time, and by encouraging authors to avoid making them, authors can save time in future efforts. @17,39322,694,61
text For example, a element's attribute causes the element's contents to be ignored. However, this isn't obvious, especially if the element's contents appear to be executable script — which can lead to authors spending a lot of time trying to debug the inline script without realizing that it is not executing. To reduce this problem, this specification makes it non-conforming to have executable script in a element when the attribute is present. This means that authors who are validating their documents are less likely to waste time with this kind of mistake. @17,39395,694,122
link script scripting.html#the-script-element @17,39529,694,20
link src scripting.html#attr-script-src @17,39561,694,20
link script scripting.html#the-script-element @17,39593,694,21
link src scripting.html#attr-script-src @17,39626,694,20
text Errors that involve areas that affect authors migrating between the HTML and XML syntaxes @17,39658,694,20
text Some authors like to write files that can be interpreted as both XML and HTML with similar results. Though this practice is discouraged in general due to the myriad of subtle complications involved (especially when involving scripting, styling, or any kind of automated serialization), this specification has a few restrictions intended to at least somewhat mitigate the difficulties. This makes it easier for authors to use this as a transitionary step when migrating between the HTML and XML syntaxes. @17,39690,694,102
text For example, there are somewhat complicated rules surrounding the and attributes intended to keep the two synchronized. @17,39804,694,40
row @17,39856,694,21
  link lang dom.html#attr-lang @17,39856,29,21
  link xml:lang https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#sec-lang-tag @58,39856,56,21
text Another example would be the restrictions on the values of xmlns attributes in the HTML serialization, which are intended to ensure that elements in conforming documents end up in the same namespaces whether processed as HTML or XML. @17,39889,694,61
text Errors that involve areas reserved for future expansion @17,39962,694,20
text As with the restrictions on the syntax intended to allow for new syntax in future revisions of the language, some restrictions on the content models of elements and values of attributes are intended to allow for future expansion of the HTML vocabulary. @17,39994,694,61
text For example, limiting the values of the attribute that start with an U+005F LOW LINE character (_) to only specific predefined values allows new predefined values to be introduced at a future time without conflicting with author-defined values. @17,40067,694,61
link target links.html#attr-hyperlink-target @17,40140,694,20
text Errors that indicate a mis-use of other specifications @17,40172,694,20
text Certain restrictions are intended to support the restrictions made by other specifications. @17,40204,694,21
text For example, requiring that attributes that take media query lists use only valid media query lists reinforces the importance of following the conformance rules of that specification. @17,40237,694,40
h3 1.12 Suggested reading @17,40289,694,19
text This section is non-normative. @17,40320,694,20
text The following documents might be of interest to readers of this specification. @17,40352,694,21
row @17,40385,694,20
  text Character Model for the World Wide Web 1.0: Fundamentals @17,40385,398,20
  link [CHARMOD] references.html#refsCHARMOD @427,40385,83,20
text This Architectural Specification provides authors of specifications, software developers, and content developers with a common reference for interoperable text manipulation on the World Wide Web, building on the Universal Character Set, defined jointly by the Unicode Standard and ISO/IEC 10646. Topics addressed include use of the terms 'character', 'encoding' and 'string', a reference processing model, choice and identification of character encodings, character escaping, and string indexing. @17,40417,694,101
row @17,40530,694,21
  text Unicode Security Considerations @17,40530,218,21
  link [UTR36] references.html#refsUTR36 @247,40530,57,21
text Because Unicode contains such a large number of characters and incorporates the varied writing systems of the world, incorrect usage can expose programs or systems to possible security attacks. This is especially important as more and more products are internationalized. This document describes some of the security considerations that programmers, system analysts, standards developers, and users should take into account, and provides specific recommendations to reduce the risk of problems. @17,40563,694,101
row @17,40676,694,20
  text Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) @17,40676,306,20
  link [WCAG] references.html#refsWCAG @335,40676,55,20
text Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) covers a wide range of recommendations for making web content more accessible. Following these guidelines will make content accessible to a wider range of people with disabilities, including blindness and low vision, deafness and hearing loss, learning disabilities, cognitive limitations, limited movement, speech disabilities, photosensitivity and combinations of these. Following these guidelines will also often make your web content more usable to users in general. @17,40708,694,122
row @17,40842,694,21
  text Authoring Tool Accessibility Guidelines (ATAG) 2.0 @17,40842,337,21
  link [ATAG] references.html#refsATAG @366,40842,50,21
text This specification provides guidelines for designing web content authoring tools that are more accessible for people with disabilities. An authoring tool that conforms to these guidelines will promote accessibility by providing an accessible user interface to authors with disabilities as well as by enabling, supporting, and promoting the production of accessible web content by all authors. @17,40875,694,81
row @17,40968,694,20
  text User Agent Accessibility Guidelines (UAAG) 2.0 @17,40968,315,20
  link [UAAG] references.html#refsUAAG @344,40968,51,20
text This document provides guidelines for designing user agents that lower barriers to web accessibility for people with disabilities. User agents include browsers and other types of software that retrieve and render web content. A user agent that conforms to these guidelines will promote accessibility through its own user interface and through other internal facilities, including its ability to communicate with other technologies (especially assistive technologies). Furthermore, all users, not just users with disabilities, should find conforming user agents to be more usable. @17,41000,694,122
